Description: CVE-2026-50251 - Attacker supplied 0.0.0.0/:: glue triggers defensive full-cache flush
 Attacker-supplied glue records pointing at the "any" / unspecified
 addresses (0.0.0.0/8 for IPv4, :: and the IPv4-mapped equivalents for
 IPv6) are, on Linux, routed to the local host. Unbound would attempt to
 query such addresses, and the resulting defensive handling could trigger
 a full cache flush, giving a remote attacker a denial-of-service /
 cache-poisoning aid vector (CWE-184: incomplete list of disallowed
 inputs).
 .
 This backports the upstream fix by extending the built-in
 do-not-query-address list in donotq_apply_cfg() to also cover
 0.0.0.0/8, ::, ::ffff:0:0/96 and ::ffff:127.0.0.0/104, so glue pointing
 at these addresses is never queried. The change is purely additive and
 applies unchanged against the target's donotq_apply_cfg().
 .
 A regression test (testdata/iter_donotq_zero.rpl) is added, adapted from
 the target's existing iter_donotq127.rpl fixture, exercising the new
 0.0.0.0 glue blocking path (the upstream fixture dns_error_reporting.rpl
 relies on the RFC 9567 DNS Error Reporting feature that does not exist in
 this target version and is therefore not portable).
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/NLnetLabs/unbound/commit/e180b06298d8d39a764d3c5d4d4aca472c3a97d7
Author: W.C.A. Wijngaards <wouter@nlnetlabs.nl>
Last-Update: 2026-07-31
---
--- a/iterator/iter_donotq.c
+++ b/iterator/iter_donotq.c
@@ -132,6 +132,18 @@ donotq_apply_cfg(struct iter_donotq* dq, struct config_file* cfg)
 		if(cfg->do_ip6) {
 			if(!donotq_str_cfg(dq, "::1"))
 				return 0;
+			if(!donotq_str_cfg(dq, "::ffff:127.0.0.0/104"))
+				return 0;
+		}
+		/* RFC 1122 3.2.1.3 / RFC 6890 / RFC 4291 2.5.2: not valid as
+		 * destination; on Linux these route to the local host. */
+		if(!donotq_str_cfg(dq, "0.0.0.0/8"))
+			return 0;
+		if(cfg->do_ip6) {
+			if(!donotq_str_cfg(dq, "::"))
+				return 0;
+			if(!donotq_str_cfg(dq, "::ffff:0:0/96"))
+				return 0;
 		}
 	}
 	addr_tree_init_parents(&dq->tree);
