Description: CVE-2026-50046 - Fix heap use-after-free in DoT forwarded query error path
 A possible heap use-after-free could occur in an error path when a DoT
 forwarded query is jostled out. The waiting_tcp structure stored a bare
 reference to the tls_auth_name owned by the serviced_query. When the
 serviced_query was freed while the waiting_tcp still lived (e.g. jostled
 out on an error path), the dangling pointer could be used after free.
 This change makes waiting_tcp own a strdup'd copy of tls_auth_name and
 frees it in waiting_tcp_delete().
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/NLnetLabs/unbound/commit/1ad8d4c39594dcb28d636fb4922737a3640c9a65
Author: W.C.A. Wijngaards <wouter@nlnetlabs.nl>
Last-Update: 2026-07-28
---
--- a/services/outside_network.c
+++ b/services/outside_network.c
@@ -195,6 +195,7 @@
 waiting_tcp_delete(struct waiting_tcp* w)
 {
 	if(!w) return;
+	free(w->tls_auth_name);
 	if(w->timer)
 		comm_timer_delete(w->timer);
 	free(w);
@@ -2474,7 +2475,16 @@
 	w->cb = callback;
 	w->cb_arg = callback_arg;
 	w->ssl_upstream = sq->ssl_upstream;
-	w->tls_auth_name = sq->tls_auth_name;
+	if(sq->tls_auth_name) {
+		w->tls_auth_name = strdup(sq->tls_auth_name);
+		if(!w->tls_auth_name) {
+			comm_timer_delete(w->timer);
+			free(w);
+			return NULL;
+		}
+	} else {
+		w->tls_auth_name = NULL;
+	}
 	w->timeout = timeout;
 	w->id_node.key = NULL;
 	w->write_wait_prev = NULL;
--- a/services/outside_network.h
+++ b/services/outside_network.h
@@ -412,7 +412,7 @@
 	void* cb_arg;
 	/** if it uses ssl upstream */
 	int ssl_upstream;
-	/** ref to the tls_auth_name from the serviced_query */
+	/** owned copy of the tls_auth_name (malloced) */
 	char* tls_auth_name;
 	/** the packet was involved in an error, to stop looping errors */
 	int error_count;
Description: CVE-2026-32665 - Fix remote DNS-over-QUIC denial of service due to quic-size budget bypass
 When compiled with DoQ support (--with-libngtcp2) and quic-port configured,
 the per-stream quic-size memory budget could be bypassed. The first two
 bidirectional streams on a new QUIC connection (stream_id 0 and 4) skipped
 the per-stream quic-size gate entirely, and large buffers were allocated after
 receiving only a 2-byte length prefix. Using one connection and two streams
 declaring 65535-byte length prefixes, a remote attacker could force Unbound to
 allocate roughly double the configured memory budget, causing denial of service
 for new DoQ clients.
 .
 This backports the upstream fix: the unconditional bypass for stream_id 0 and 4
 in doq_stream_open_cb() is removed, and the quic-size availability is now checked
 in doq_stream_datalen_complete() before allocating the incoming query buffer and
 in doq_stream_pickup_answer() before allocating the reply buffer. The connection
 pointer is threaded through doq_stream_recv_data(), doq_stream_datalen_complete()
 and doq_stream_pickup_answer() to reach the socket configuration.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/NLnetLabs/unbound/commit/01dfd2f466d383370405d8ecf939570947f3523e
Bug: https://www.nlnetlabs.nl/downloads/unbound/CVE-2026-32665.txt
